// =============================================================================
// How to perform every imported gym exercise.
//
// The bulk catalogue arrived with a name, a muscle list and a one-line note on
// what each lift is FOR — which the app was printing under a heading that said
// "How to perform it". 245 exercises that could not teach you the movement.
//
// Each entry here is the same shape on purpose: how to set up, how to execute,
// and the single mistake that most often makes the lift useless or dangerous.
// That last clause is the one that matters. Anyone can copy a rep from a video;
// what people get wrong is always the same handful of things.
//
// Written rather than generated. Bad form advice on a loaded barbell hurts
// people, so every line here is a claim someone is accountable for.
// =============================================================================

export const IMPORTED_HOWTO: Record<string, string> = {
  // --- the ones athletes typed in themselves ---------------------------------
  // Off a report of custom log entries, which is the most honest gap list there
  // is: somebody wanted the exercise, could not find it, and wrote it anyway.
  // A row that opens onto "what it's for" instead of how to do it is worse than
  // the search that failed to find it.
  x_incline_bicep_curl: "Set a bench to about forty-five degrees, sit back into it and let the arms hang straight down behind the line of your body. That hanging start is the whole exercise — it holds the long head of the biceps at length, which a standing curl never does. Curl without letting the elbows travel forward, squeeze at the top, and lower all the way until the arm is straight again. It will feel much harder than a standing curl at the same weight; go lighter and do not cheat the bottom half.",
  x_rear_delt_fly: "Hinge at the hips until your chest is close to facing the floor, with a soft knee and a flat back. Hold a light pair of dumbbells hanging under your shoulders, then sweep the arms apart in a wide arc, leading with the elbows rather than the hands. Stop when the arms are level with the shoulders — going higher hands the work to the traps. Lower under control. The weight that works here is far lighter than people expect, because the rear delt is small and every extra kilo recruits the back instead.",
  x_cable_chest_fly: "Set the pulleys at shoulder height, take a handle in each hand and stand a stride in front of the stack so there is tension before you start. Keep a soft, fixed elbow throughout — the angle should not change — and bring the hands all the way together in front of the chest rather than just inward. Let the chest stretch as the arms open behind you. The cable is the point: at the top of a dumbbell fly the weight is almost resting against gravity, and here it never stops pulling.",
  x_single_arm_tricep_extension: "Hold one dumbbell overhead with the upper arm vertical and beside your head. Lower the weight behind your head, not out to the side, keeping the upper arm still — only the elbow moves. Extend back to lockout without swinging or letting the shoulder drift forward. Working one arm at a time is deliberate: with two hands the stronger side quietly takes more of the load and you never find out, which is exactly how an imbalance survives months of training.",
  x_skull_crushers: "Lie on a flat bench holding an EZ bar or dumbbells with the arms straight above the chest. Keep the elbows tucked in and pointing at the ceiling — they should not flare or drift back — and bend at the elbow to lower the weight to your forehead or just past it. Press back up, stopping just short of full lockout so the triceps stay loaded. Because the arms stay overhead the long head is worked at length, which a pushdown does not reach.",

  // --- Horizontal press ------------------------------------------------------
  // --- the lifts the programme is anchored on -------------------------------
  // Added with the staple lifts themselves. These are now the first movement of
  // most sessions the engine writes, and a block that opens on a barbell lift
  // and cannot tell you how to perform it is teaching nobody anything.
  x_barbell_back_squat: "Set the bar on the meat of your traps, not on the bone at the base of the neck. Take it out of the rack with both feet under you rather than walking it out step by step, get a wide brace of air into the belly, and sit between your hips — knees tracking over the toes, not caving in. Go to at least parallel if your hips and ankles allow it, then drive the floor away with the chest and hips rising together. Brace before you unrack, not on the way down: the most common failure is losing the trunk position under a bar that is already moving.",
  x_barbell_front_squat: "Rest the bar across the front of the shoulders and hold it there with the elbows high — the hands are a shelf, not a grip, so a couple of fingers is plenty. Keep the elbows up for every inch of the rep: the moment the chest drops, the bar rolls forward and you lose it. Sit straight down with an upright torso to full depth, then drive up. It will feel lighter than a back squat and it is not — the limit is how long you can hold the rack position.",
  x_barbell_deadlift: "Stand with the bar over the middle of your foot, shins almost touching it. Hinge down and take your grip just outside the legs, drop the hips until the shins meet the bar, and pull your chest up so the back is flat. Take the slack out of the bar — you should hear it click into the plates — then push the floor away. Hips and chest rise together; if the hips shoot up first, the bar drifts forward and your lower back finishes the lift. Reset your position for every rep rather than bouncing off the floor.",
  x_barbell_overhead_press: "Bar on the front of the shoulders, hands just outside shoulder width, feet under the hips. Squeeze the glutes and brace so the ribs stay down — arching the lower back is how a press turns into a standing incline press. Move the head back an inch, press the bar straight past the face, then push the head back through so it finishes over the middle of the foot with the biceps by the ears. No leg drive: if you have to dip, the weight is too heavy.",
  x_barbell_row: "Hinge forward to about 45° with a flat back and soft knees, bar hanging at arm's length. Row it to the bottom of the ribs, driving the elbows back rather than pulling with the hands, and squeeze the shoulder blades at the top. Lower under control to a full stretch. The give-away fault is the torso rising as the bar does — if you are standing up to move it, the weight is doing the choosing.",
  x_pull_ups: "Hang at full stretch from a bar with an overhand grip a little wider than the shoulders. Start by pulling the shoulder blades down, then drive the elbows down and back to bring the chest towards the bar. Lower all the way to a dead hang between reps. Kipping and half-range reps make the numbers look better and train less — if you cannot get five clean ones, use a band or do the lowering half only.",
  x_leg_press: "Feet on the middle of the platform, about shoulder width, toes turned slightly out. Release the safeties and lower until the knees reach around 90° or until your hips start to tuck under — that tuck is the point where the lower back takes over, and going past it is where people hurt themselves. Press back up without slamming the knees into a hard lockout. Higher feet shift the work to the glutes and hamstrings, lower feet to the quads.",
  x_cable_tricep_pushdown: "Face the cable with a high pulley, elbows pinned against the ribs and a slight forward lean. Extend the elbows to a full lockout and squeeze, then let the weight pull the hands back up until you feel the triceps stretch — without letting the elbows drift forward. The whole movement happens below the elbow; if the shoulders are helping, drop the weight.",
  x_leg_curl: "Lie or sit with the pad just above the heels and the hips flat against the bench. Curl as far as the machine allows and hold for a beat at the top. Lower slowly — the lengthening half is where most of the hamstring growth comes from, so a three-second return is worth more than extra weight. Letting the hips lift off the pad shortens the range and hands the work to the lower back.",
  x_plank: "Forearms under the shoulders, feet hip width, body in one line from head to heels. Squeeze the glutes and pull the ribs down towards the hips so the lower back does not sag — that brace is the exercise, not the holding on. Breathe normally throughout. When the hips start to drop or lift, the set is over: two good thirty-second holds beat one sloppy two-minute one.",

  x_bench_press: "Lie with your eyes under the bar, feet flat, and pull your shoulder blades back and down into the bench. Take a grip a little wider than shoulder width, unrack, and lower the bar under control to the lower chest with elbows at roughly 45° to the body. Press back over the shoulders. Flaring the elbows straight out to the sides is what wrecks shoulders — keep them tucked.",
  x_dumbbell_bench_press: "Sit with the dumbbells on your thighs, then kick them up as you lie back. Shoulder blades pinned, lower until your upper arms are level with the torso, then press up and slightly together without clashing the bells. Dumbbells allow a deeper stretch than a bar, which is the point — don't cut the range short.",
  x_incline_bench_press: "Set the bench to 30–45°; steeper turns it into a shoulder press. Same setup as a flat bench — shoulder blades retracted, feet planted — and lower the bar to the upper chest just below the collarbone. Press back up over the shoulders. Too steep an incline is the usual mistake and takes the chest out of it.",
  x_incline_dumbbell_bench_press: "Bench at 30–45°, dumbbells kicked up from the thighs. Lower until you feel a stretch across the upper chest, elbows about 45° from the body, then press up and slightly in. Control the lowering — the stretched position is where the upper chest actually gets trained.",
  x_decline_bench_press: "Lock your legs into the pads first so you're secure before unracking. Lower the bar to the lower chest and press straight up. The decline shortens the range and takes the shoulders out, so it usually feels stronger than a flat bench — don't let that tempt you into a weight you can't control to the chest.",
  x_decline_dumbbell_bench_press: "Secure your legs, then kick the dumbbells up. Lower to the lower chest with control and press up and slightly together. Getting the dumbbells into position safely is the hardest part — have a partner hand them to you if they're heavy.",
  x_close_grip_bench_press: "Grip roughly shoulder width — not narrower, which strains the wrists for no extra triceps. Keep the elbows tucked close to the ribs and lower the bar to the lower chest, then press. The tucked elbow is the whole exercise; let them flare and it becomes an ordinary bench press.",
  x_close_grip_dumbbell_bench_press: "Hold the dumbbells with a neutral grip, palms facing each other, and keep them touching or nearly touching throughout. Lower to the chest with elbows tucked, then press. The neutral grip is much kinder to the shoulder than a flat press, which is why this is a good substitute if benching aggravates yours.",
  x_wide_grip_bench_press: "Take a grip wider than your normal bench, lower the bar to the upper chest, and press. The wider grip shortens the range and biases the chest, but it puts the shoulder in a more vulnerable position — go lighter than your standard bench and stop if you feel any pinching at the front of the shoulder.",
  x_reverse_grip_bench_press: "Take an underhand grip, slightly wider than shoulder width, and have a spotter help you unrack — the bar is much harder to control coming out of the rack this way. Lower to the lower chest with elbows tucked and press. It biases the upper chest and is easy on the shoulder, but only with a competent unrack.",
  x_paused_bench_press: "Bench as normal, but stop with the bar resting motionless on the chest for a full second before pressing. Stay tight through the pause — the temptation is to relax, and that's exactly what you're training out. It removes the bounce entirely, so expect to use less weight than your touch-and-go bench.",
  x_spoto_press: "Lower the bar to about an inch above your chest, hold it there for a second without touching, then press. Because nothing touches, you have to control the weight in the hardest position for the full pause. Excellent for a sticking point just off the chest; use around 80% of your normal bench.",
  x_floor_press: "Lie on the floor with knees bent, unrack or have the bar handed to you, and lower until your upper arms rest on the floor. Pause briefly, then press. The floor stops the range early, which spares the shoulder and overloads the top half of the press — that pause is a genuine stop, not a bounce.",
  x_dumbbell_floor_press: "Lie on the floor with dumbbells at the chest, knees bent. Lower until your triceps touch the floor, pause, then press up. The shortened range makes this the go-to press when a shoulder is unhappy with full-range benching.",
  x_bench_pin_press: "Set the safety pins in the rack at the height you want to press from, lie down, and press the bar off a dead stop each rep, returning it to the pins between reps. Starting from dead stop kills all momentum and exposes exactly where you're weak — do not bounce the bar off the pins.",
  x_smith_machine_bench_press: "Set the bench so the bar lands on your lower chest, unhook, and press in the machine's fixed path. The fixed bar means you can push closer to failure alone, but it also removes the stabilising work — treat it as a supplement to free-weight pressing, not a replacement.",
  x_chest_press: "Sit with your back flat against the pad and the handles level with your mid-chest — adjust the seat until they are. Press forward until the arms are almost straight without locking hard, then return under control until you feel a stretch. Setting the seat too high turns it into a shoulder press.",
  x_machine_chest_fly: "Set the seat so the handles sit level with your chest and your elbows have a slight, fixed bend. Bring the handles together in an arc, squeeze for a beat, then let them open until you feel a stretch across the chest. The elbow angle stays constant — bending and straightening turns a fly into a press.",
  x_dumbbell_fly: "Lie on a flat bench holding dumbbells above the chest with a soft, fixed bend in the elbows. Open the arms out in a wide arc until you feel a stretch across the chest, then bring them back together as if hugging a barrel. Go lighter than you think — this is a stretch exercise and the shoulder is exposed at the bottom.",
  x_incline_dumbbell_fly: "Bench at 30°, dumbbells above the upper chest, elbows softly bent and fixed. Open out until you feel a stretch, then arc back together. The incline shifts the work to the upper chest. Never turn it into a press by bending the elbows to get through a heavy set.",
  x_decline_dumbbell_fly: "Legs secured, dumbbells above the lower chest with a fixed soft elbow bend. Open in a wide arc, feel the stretch, then arc back together. Keep the movement slow — momentum at the bottom of any fly is where pec tears happen.",
  x_cable_fly: "Set both pulleys high (or low for an upward fly), take a handle in each hand, and step forward into a split stance with a slight forward lean. With a fixed soft elbow bend, bring the handles together in front of you and squeeze. Cables keep tension on the chest at the top, which dumbbells lose — pause there.",

  // --- Vertical press --------------------------------------------------------
  x_shoulder_press: "Stand or sit with the bar at collarbone height, hands just outside the shoulders, elbows slightly in front of the bar. Brace the trunk and glutes, then press overhead, moving your head back out of the way and pushing it through once the bar passes. Finish with the bar over the mid-foot and biceps by the ears.",
  x_military_press: "A strict standing press, feet together or hip width. No leg drive at all — brace hard, press the bar past your face, and lock out overhead with the ribs down. Leaning back to get the bar up is the universal fault and it loads the lower back instead of the shoulders.",
  x_seated_shoulder_press: "Sit with your back against the upright, bar at collarbone height. Press overhead, pushing the head through at lockout. Seated removes leg drive entirely so it's stricter than standing, but it also pins the lower back — don't arch over the bench to force out extra reps.",
  x_seated_dumbbell_shoulder_press: "Sit with back supported, dumbbells at ear height and palms forward. Press up and slightly together without clashing them, then lower under control until the elbows are just below shoulder height. Dumbbells let the shoulders move naturally, so this is usually kinder than a bar if pressing bothers you.",
  x_machine_shoulder_press: "Adjust the seat so the handles start at about shoulder height. Press up smoothly to near-lockout and lower under control. The fixed path means you can train close to failure safely on your own, which is its real advantage over free weights.",
  x_arnold_press: "Start with dumbbells at chest height, palms facing you. Press up while rotating the palms to face forward, finishing overhead. Reverse the rotation exactly on the way down. The rotation brings in the front and side delts together — do it smoothly rather than twisting at the last moment.",
  x_push_press: "Set up as for a strict press. Dip a few inches by bending the knees with the torso vertical, then drive explosively through the legs and use that momentum to punch the bar overhead, locking out with the arms. The dip is short and sharp — dipping too deep turns it into a squat and kills the drive.",
  x_push_jerk: "Dip and drive as for a push press, but as the bar leaves the shoulders, drop back under it by re-bending the knees and catch it locked out overhead in a partial squat. Then stand up. The skill is receiving the bar with straight arms rather than pressing it out.",
  x_split_jerk: "Dip and drive the bar up, then split the feet — one forward, one back — dropping under to catch it locked out overhead. Front shin vertical, back knee soft, torso upright and the bar over the middle of your base. Recover the front foot first, then the back. Catching with a soft or bent arm is the common failure.",
  x_behind_the_neck_press: "Only attempt this with genuinely good overhead shoulder mobility. Take a wide grip, lower the bar behind the head to about ear level, and press back up. Never go below the ears. If you can't keep the torso upright without straining the shoulder, use a normal overhead press instead — there's no benefit worth the risk.",
  x_z_press: "Sit on the floor with legs straight in front of you and the bar at your collarbone. With no back support and no leg drive available, press overhead. The trunk has to do everything, which is the point — if you're collapsing or leaning back, the weight is too heavy.",
  x_dumbbell_z_press: "Seated on the floor, legs straight, dumbbells at shoulder height. Press overhead while staying tall through the spine. Brutally honest about core and shoulder stability, so start much lighter than your usual seated press.",
  x_handstand_push_ups: "Kick up into a handstand against a wall, hands slightly wider than shoulders. Lower under control until the head lightly touches the floor, then press back to lockout. Build to this with pike push-ups first — dropping onto your head because you fatigued mid-rep is the risk.",
  x_pike_push_up: "From a push-up position, walk your feet in and lift the hips high so your body forms an inverted V. Bend the elbows to lower the crown of your head toward the floor, then press back up. The closer your feet are to your hands, the more vertical and the harder it becomes.",
  x_log_press: "Take a neutral grip on the log handles, clean it to the chest, then dip and drive it overhead, pushing the head through at lockout. The thick neutral grip and the log's distance from the body make it far harder than a barbell press — expect noticeably less weight.",
  x_viking_press: "Take the neutral-grip handles at shoulder height, brace, and press overhead following the machine's arc. The fixed path and neutral grip usually let you press more than a barbell and are easier on the shoulders.",
  x_landmine_press: "With one end of a barbell in a landmine (or corner), hold the other end at your shoulder. Press up and slightly forward following the bar's natural arc, extending the arm fully. The angled path is much friendlier to the shoulder than a strict vertical press.",
  x_one_arm_landmine_press: "Hold the bar end at one shoulder in a half-kneeling or standing stance. Press up and across, letting the torso resist the rotation the bar wants to create. Fighting that rotation is half the exercise — keep the hips square.",

  // --- Vertical pull ---------------------------------------------------------
  x_chin_ups: "Hang from a bar with an underhand grip about shoulder width, arms fully straight. Pull your chest toward the bar, driving the elbows down and back, until your chin clears it. Lower all the way to a dead hang. The underhand grip brings the biceps in heavily — that's why most people can do more chins than pull-ups.",
  x_neutral_grip_pull_ups: "Hang from parallel handles, palms facing each other. Pull until your chin passes the handles, leading with the chest and driving the elbows down. Lower under control to a full hang. The neutral grip is the most shoulder-friendly of the three and the best choice if wide-grip pull-ups pinch.",
  x_muscle_ups: "From a hang, pull explosively as high as you can while leaning back, then whip the chest over the bar and transition the wrists on top before pressing to lockout. It needs a chest-height pull-up and a strong dip before it's worth attempting — most failed muscle-ups are simply a pull that wasn't high enough.",
  x_ring_muscle_ups: "From a false grip on the rings, pull hard and high, then roll the shoulders over the rings and press out to lockout with the rings turned out. The false grip is the prerequisite and takes weeks to build — trying it without one is why the transition stalls.",
  x_clap_pull_up: "From a dead hang, pull explosively enough that your chest reaches the bar and your hands can leave it. Clap, then catch the bar and absorb the drop with slightly bent arms. Only attempt with a solid base of strict pull-ups; catching with locked arms is how elbows get hurt.",
  x_close_grip_lat_pulldown: "Sit with thighs locked under the pads, take a narrow or V-handle grip, and pull the handle to the upper chest, leading with the elbows down and back. Lean back only slightly and hold it there. Let the weight stretch the lats fully at the top rather than stopping halfway.",
  x_reverse_grip_lat_pulldown: "Take an underhand grip about shoulder width, thighs secured, and pull the bar to the upper chest with the elbows driving down close to the ribs. The underhand grip brings the lower lats and biceps in. Keep the chest tall rather than rounding toward the bar.",
  x_one_arm_lat_pulldown: "Attach a single handle, kneel or sit facing the machine, and pull with one arm, letting the shoulder stretch up fully at the top. Pull the elbow down toward your hip. Working one side at a time exposes a strength difference the bar version hides.",
  x_straight_arm_pulldown: "Stand facing a high pulley, take a bar with straight (but not locked) arms, and pull it down to your thighs in an arc, keeping the arms straight throughout. It isolates the lats without the biceps. Bending the elbows turns it into a pulldown and defeats the purpose.",

  // --- Horizontal pull -------------------------------------------------------
  x_bent_over_row: "Hinge at the hips until the torso is around 45° or lower, back flat, bar hanging at arm's length. Row to the lower ribs, driving the elbows back, then lower under control. The torso angle must not change during the set — standing up as you row is how this turns into a shrug and a sore back.",
  x_pendlay_row: "Set the torso parallel to the floor and row the bar explosively from the floor to the lower chest, then return it to the floor completely between every rep. Each rep starts dead. It's stricter than a bent-over row and far harder to cheat, which is the point.",
  x_yates_row: "Stand more upright than a bent-over row — around 20–30° of forward lean — take an underhand grip, and row to the lower abdomen. The upright angle allows heavier loading and the underhand grip brings the lower lats in. Keep it strict rather than turning it into a jerk.",
  x_dumbbell_row: "With one knee and hand on a bench, hold a dumbbell at arm's length with a flat back. Row it to your hip, driving the elbow back and past the ribs, then lower to a full stretch. Rotating the torso to help the weight up is the standard cheat — keep the shoulders square to the floor.",
  x_chest_supported_dumbbell_row: "Lie chest-down on an incline bench with dumbbells hanging beneath you. Row them to your ribs, squeezing the shoulder blades together, then lower fully. Because the bench removes all cheating and takes the lower back out, this is the cleanest row there is for building the mid-back.",
  x_t_bar_row: "Straddle the bar, chest against the pad or hinged forward with a flat back, and take the handles. Row to the abdomen, driving the elbows back and squeezing at the top, then lower to a full stretch. Heaving with the hips is the usual fault.",
  x_meadows_row: "Stand side-on to a landmine bar, hinge at the hips and take the bar end with the outside hand, other hand braced on your knee. Row up and back, letting the shoulder stretch forward at the bottom. The angle gives an unusually long range through the lat.",
  x_seated_cable_row: "Sit with a slight knee bend and a tall chest, arms extended. Pull the handle to your abdomen, driving the elbows back and squeezing the shoulder blades, then let the arms extend fully and allow the shoulder blades to spread at the front. Rocking back and forth with the torso is the thing to eliminate.",
  x_one_arm_seated_cable_row: "Same setup with a single handle. Pull to the side of your abdomen, allowing the shoulder to travel forward at the stretch and back at the finish. One side at a time gives more range and shows up an imbalance.",
  x_machine_row: "Set the chest pad so the handles are reachable with arms extended. Row back, driving the elbows past the torso and squeezing the shoulder blades, then return under control to a full stretch. The chest pad takes the lower back out entirely — useful on a heavy leg week.",
  x_inverted_row: "Set a bar at roughly hip height, hang underneath with a straight body and heels on the floor. Pull your chest to the bar, keeping the body rigid from head to heels, then lower under control. Raise the bar to make it easier, lower it to make it harder. Letting the hips sag is the most common fault.",
  x_bench_pull: "Lie chest-down on a high bench with a barbell beneath you. Row it to the underside of the bench, then lower to full extension. Because the bench takes the lower back and any body English out, this is the honest measure of your rowing strength.",
  x_renegade_row: "Start in a push-up position gripping two dumbbells, feet wide for stability. Row one dumbbell to the hip while keeping the hips absolutely square to the floor, then swap. The anti-rotation demand is the exercise — if your hips are twisting, go lighter.",
  x_face_pull: "Set a rope at upper-chest height. Pull it toward your face, separating your hands and finishing with the elbows high and wide and the hands beside your ears. Squeeze the rear delts. Pulling low into the chest is the usual mistake and misses the muscle entirely.",
  x_dumbbell_face_pull: "Hinge forward, dumbbells hanging, then pull them up toward your face with elbows high and wide, rotating the shoulders back at the top. Light weight and a hard squeeze — this is a postural exercise, not a strength one.",
  x_dumbbell_reverse_fly: "Hinge at the hips with a flat back, dumbbells hanging beneath your chest with a soft elbow bend. Raise them out to the sides in a wide arc, squeezing the shoulder blades, then lower under control. Very light weight; shrugging the traps up is the fault to avoid.",
  x_machine_reverse_fly: "Sit chest against the pad, take the handles, and sweep the arms out and back in a wide arc, squeezing between the shoulder blades. Keep the elbows soft and fixed. Control the return rather than letting the stack drop.",
  x_cable_reverse_fly: "Set two pulleys at shoulder height and cross the cables, taking the left handle in the right hand and vice versa. Sweep the arms out and back in a wide arc, squeezing the rear delts. Cables keep tension right through the movement, which dumbbells lose at the bottom.",

  // --- Squat -----------------------------------------------------------------
  x_bodyweight_squat: "Feet about shoulder width, toes slightly out. Sit back and down, keeping the chest tall and the knees tracking over the toes, until the thighs are at least parallel. Drive back up through the whole foot. Master this pattern before adding any load — everything else is built on it.",
  x_dumbbell_squat: "Hold a dumbbell in each hand at your sides, or one at the chest. Feet shoulder width, sit down and back until the thighs are parallel or lower, then drive up. Keep the chest tall and the weight through the mid-foot. A good way to squat without a rack.",
  x_dumbbell_front_squat: "Hold two dumbbells at shoulder height, elbows up. Squat down while keeping the chest tall and the elbows high — the moment the elbows drop, the torso follows and the weight shifts forward. Drive up through the mid-foot.",
  x_hack_squat: "In the machine, feet mid-platform about shoulder width, back and shoulders against the pads. Release the safeties and lower until the knees reach about 90° or slightly past, then drive up without locking out hard. Foot position changes the emphasis — higher hits the glutes and hamstrings, lower the quads.",
  x_barbell_hack_squat: "Stand with a barbell on the floor behind your heels. Squat down, grip it behind you, and stand up, keeping the bar close to your legs throughout. It's a quad-biased deadlift variant that predates the machine — keep the torso as upright as your mobility allows.",
  x_smith_machine_squat: "Set the bar on your upper back and position your feet slightly forward of the bar. Unlock and squat to at least parallel, then drive up. The fixed path lets you push closer to failure safely, but it also means your body must fit the machine's line — if it feels like your knees or back are being forced, use a free bar.",
  x_box_squat: "Set a box so that sitting on it puts you at or just below parallel. Squat back onto it, pause with the weight fully settled but the trunk still braced, then drive up. It teaches sitting back rather than down. Do not rock or bounce off the box.",
  x_pause_squat: "Squat to depth and hold motionless at the bottom for two to three seconds before driving up. Stay braced through the pause — relaxing is what makes the next rep dangerous. It removes the stretch reflex entirely and exposes weakness out of the hole.",
  x_pin_squat: "Set safety pins at your target depth, squat down until the bar rests on them, release the tension for a beat, then drive up from a dead stop. The hardest squat variant there is for a given weight, and the most honest about a sticking point.",
  x_half_squat: "Squat to roughly a quarter or half of full depth and drive back up. Useful for overloading the top range for sport, but it should supplement full-depth squatting rather than replace it — half squats with big weights and no full-range work is how knees get unhappy.",
  x_sumo_squat: "Take a wide stance with toes turned out around 45°. Squat straight down between your legs, keeping the chest tall and the knees tracking out over the toes. The wide stance brings the adductors and glutes in heavily.",
  x_zercher_squat: "Hold the bar in the crooks of your elbows, arms tucked in. Squat with the chest tall — the front-loaded position forces a very upright torso and punishes any forward lean. It's uncomfortable on the arms; use a towel or sleeves and start light.",
  x_safety_bar_squat: "With the safety bar's yoke on your shoulders and hands on the handles, squat as normal. The bar's design pushes you forward slightly, so brace the upper back hard. It's the answer when shoulder mobility makes holding a straight bar painful.",
  x_belt_squat: "Attach the belt around your hips, stand on the platform, and squat with the load hanging from the hips instead of the spine. Because nothing sits on your back, it's the way to train the legs hard when the lower back is beaten up or injured.",
  x_landmine_squat: "Hold the end of a landmine bar at chest height, feet shoulder width. Squat down and up — the bar's arc naturally counterbalances you, which keeps the torso upright and makes it one of the easiest ways to teach a good squat pattern.",
  x_overhead_squat: "Hold the bar locked out overhead with a wide grip, actively pushing up into it. Squat to full depth keeping the bar over the mid-foot and the arms locked. It demands serious shoulder and ankle mobility — start with a broomstick and only load it once the pattern holds.",
  x_sissy_squat: "Holding a support, rise onto the balls of your feet and lean back, letting the knees travel forward as you lower, keeping hips and shoulders in a straight line. Come up the same way. Extremely demanding on the knees — build up slowly and stop if there's any joint pain.",
  x_single_leg_squat: "Stand on one leg with the other extended in front. Sit back and down as far as you can control, keeping the knee tracking over the foot, then drive up. Use a support or a box to catch you until the strength and balance are there.",
  x_pistol_squat: "Stand on one leg, other leg held straight out in front. Lower all the way to the bottom under control, then stand back up without touching down. It needs ankle mobility, single-leg strength and balance together — build it with box pistols first.",
  x_split_squat: "Take a long stride, back heel raised. Lower straight down until the back knee is just off the floor and the front shin is roughly vertical, then drive up through the front foot. Travel straight down, not forward — pushing the front knee far over the toes is the usual error.",
  x_dumbbell_split_squat: "Same as the split squat with a dumbbell in each hand. Lower straight down, front shin vertical, back knee approaching the floor, then drive through the front heel. Keeping the torso tall is what keeps the load on the front leg.",

  // --- Lunge -----------------------------------------------------------------
  x_lunge: "Step forward into a long stride and lower until both knees are around 90°, front shin roughly vertical. Push back through the front heel to the start. A short step turns it into a knee-dominant grind — step out properly.",
  x_barbell_lunge: "Bar on the upper back, step forward into a long stride and lower until the back knee nearly touches, then drive back through the front heel. The bar makes balance the limiting factor, so establish the pattern unloaded first.",
  x_dumbbell_lunge: "A dumbbell in each hand, step forward, lower until the back knee is just off the floor, then push back. The weights at your sides make this easier to balance than a barbell and easier to bail out of.",
  x_walking_lunge: "Step forward and lower until the back knee nearly touches, then drive through the front foot to bring the back leg through into the next stride. Keep the torso tall and don't let the front knee cave inward as you push off.",
  x_reverse_lunge: "Step backwards into a lunge, lower until the back knee is just off the floor, then drive through the front heel to return. Stepping back rather than forward is much kinder to the knees, which makes this the better default for most people.",
  x_barbell_reverse_lunge: "Bar racked on the upper back, step back into a lunge, lower under control, then drive through the front heel to stand. Keep the torso upright; leaning forward shifts the load off the working leg.",
  x_side_lunge: "Step wide to one side, sitting the hips back and bending that knee while the other leg stays straight. Push back to the middle. Keep the toes pointing forward and the chest tall — it trains the adductors and the lateral movement that forward lunges miss.",

  // --- Hinge -----------------------------------------------------------------
  x_romanian_deadlift: "Start standing with the bar at your thighs. Push the hips back, letting the bar slide down the legs, keeping the back flat and knees only slightly bent, until you feel a strong hamstring stretch — usually around the shins. Drive the hips forward to stand. It's a hip hinge, not a squat, and the bar stays against the legs the whole way.",
  x_dumbbell_romanian_deadlift: "Dumbbells in front of your thighs, soft knees. Push the hips back and lower the weights down the front of your legs until you feel the hamstrings stretch, then drive the hips forward to stand tall. Rounding the lower back to reach lower is the fault — stop where your hamstrings stop you.",
  x_stiff_leg_deadlift: "From the floor with legs almost straight, hinge at the hips with a flat back and lift the bar by driving the hips forward. Legs stay nearly locked throughout, which puts a huge stretch on the hamstrings — go significantly lighter than a conventional deadlift.",
  x_sumo_deadlift: "Take a wide stance with toes turned out, grip inside the knees. Drop the hips, chest up, and push the floor apart as you stand, keeping the bar against your legs. The wide stance shortens the range and keeps the torso more upright — it's a legs-and-hips pull rather than a back one.",
  x_rack_pull: "Set the pins so the bar starts just below or above the knee. Take your deadlift stance, brace, and pull to lockout, then lower back to the pins. It overloads the top half of the deadlift — resist the urge to add so much weight that the back rounds.",
  x_deficit_deadlift: "Stand on a plate or low platform so the bar starts lower than usual. Deadlift as normal. The extra range makes the start much harder and builds strength off the floor. Only do these if you can hold a flat back through the increased range.",
  x_pause_deadlift: "Pull the bar to just below the knee, pause for two seconds holding position, then complete the lift. The pause exposes any tendency for the hips to shoot up or the bar to drift away, and forces you to hold a strong position under load.",
  x_snatch_deadlift: "Take a very wide, snatch-width grip and deadlift with the hips lower and the chest higher than a conventional pull. The wide grip lengthens the range considerably. It builds the positions for the snatch as much as it builds strength.",
  x_jefferson_deadlift: "Straddle the bar with one foot either side, one hand in front and one behind you, and stand up. The asymmetric stance loads the body unevenly on purpose — do equal sets with the stance reversed, and keep the weight moderate.",
  x_zercher_deadlift: "Squat down and hook the bar into the crooks of your elbows, then stand. The front-loaded position forces an upright torso and a hard upper-back brace. Use padding — the discomfort on the arms is usually the limiting factor before the legs are.",
  x_behind_the_back_deadlift: "Stand in front of the bar and grip it behind your legs, then stand up, keeping it in contact with the back of your thighs. It keeps you more upright than a conventional pull and biases the quads. Awkward to set up — start light.",
  x_dumbbell_deadlift: "Dumbbells beside your feet or in front of your thighs. Hinge at the hips with a flat back, grip them, and stand by driving the hips forward. Dumbbells let you keep the load closer to your centre of mass, which is easier on the lower back.",
  x_single_leg_deadlift: "Stand on one leg. Hinge forward at the hip, letting the free leg extend straight behind you as a counterbalance, until your torso is roughly parallel to the floor. Return by driving the hip forward. Keep the hips level — letting the free hip open toward the ceiling is the standard fault.",
  x_single_leg_romanian_deadlift: "Holding a weight, stand on one leg and hinge forward, free leg extending behind. Lower until you feel the hamstring of the standing leg stretch, then drive the hip forward to stand. Hips stay square to the floor throughout.",
  x_single_leg_dumbbell_deadlift: "One dumbbell, usually in the opposite hand to the standing leg. Hinge at the hip, lowering the weight toward the floor while the free leg extends behind, then stand. Balance is the limit before strength is — go slow.",
  x_good_morning: "Bar on the upper back, soft knees. Push the hips back and hinge forward with a flat back until the torso is around parallel to the floor, then drive the hips forward to stand. The lower back is under real load here, so start with an empty bar and never round.",
  x_cable_pull_through: "Face away from a low pulley with a rope between your legs. Hinge at the hips, letting the rope travel back between your legs, then drive the hips forward hard to stand, squeezing the glutes. The cable teaches the hinge better than almost anything because it pulls you into the position.",
  x_barbell_glute_bridge: "Lie on your back with a padded bar over your hips, knees bent and feet flat. Drive through the heels to lift the hips until the torso and thighs are in line, squeeze the glutes hard, then lower under control. Hyperextending the lower back at the top is the fault — finish with the ribs down.",
  x_hip_extension: "Set up in the machine or on a bench with the hips free to move. Lower under control, then extend the hips to bring the torso in line with the legs, squeezing the glutes. Stop at a straight line rather than arching past it.",
  x_reverse_hyperextension: "Lie face-down on the bench with the hips at the edge and legs hanging. Raise the legs until they're in line with the torso, squeezing the glutes, then lower under control. Swinging the legs up with momentum defeats the exercise and stresses the lower back.",
  x_glute_ham_raise: "Anchor the feet and start upright with the knees on the pad. Lower the torso forward under control, resisting all the way, then pull yourself back up using the hamstrings. Brutally hard — use a band or push off lightly with the hands until the eccentric strength is there.",
  x_glute_kickback: "On all fours or in the machine, drive one leg back and up, squeezing the glute at the top, then return under control. Keep the lower back still — most people arch the back to get more range, which takes the glute out of it.",
  x_back_extension: "Set the pad at hip height, cross the arms or hold a weight at the chest. Hinge down under control, then extend back up until your body is in a straight line. Stop at straight — arching hard past it is where the lower back complains.",
  x_machine_back_extension: "Sit with the pad against the upper back, brace, then extend backwards against the resistance and return under control. Keep the movement smooth and stop at a neutral spine rather than forcing extension.",

  // --- Leg machines ----------------------------------------------------------
  x_sled_leg_press: "Sit with your back and hips flat against the pad, feet mid-platform about shoulder width. Release the safeties and lower until the knees reach around 90°, then press without locking the knees out hard. Letting the lower back round off the pad at the bottom is what injures people here — stop before that happens.",
  x_horizontal_leg_press: "Back flat against the seat, feet on the platform shoulder width. Press out to near-extension, then return under control until the knees are around 90°. Adjust the seat so you get range without the hips tucking under at the bottom.",
  x_vertical_leg_press: "Lie underneath the platform with feet up. Lower under control to around 90° at the knee and press back. The vertical loading is severe on the lower back if the hips lift, so keep the range conservative.",
  x_single_leg_press: "One foot in the centre of the platform, other foot off. Lower under control to around 90° and press back without locking. Working one leg at a time evens up a difference the two-legged press hides completely.",
  x_leg_extension: "Sit with the knee joint lined up with the machine's pivot and the pad on the lower shin. Extend the knees to straight, squeeze for a beat, then lower under control. Slamming into full extension with heavy weight is what makes knees ache — control both directions.",
  x_cable_leg_extension: "Attach an ankle cuff to a low pulley, sit or stand, and extend the knee against the resistance, then return under control. A joint-friendly alternative when the machine's fixed path doesn't suit you.",
  x_lying_leg_curl: "Lie face-down with the pad just above the heels and the knee joint at the machine's pivot. Curl the heels toward the glutes, squeeze, then lower under control. Letting the hips lift off the bench to force the last reps is the usual cheat.",
  x_seated_leg_curl: "Sit with the thigh pad locked down and the ankle pad on the lower calf. Curl the heels down and back, squeeze, and return under control. The seated position puts the hamstrings on a longer stretch than the lying version, which is why many people feel it more.",
  x_standing_leg_curl: "Standing with one leg braced, curl the working heel toward the glute, keeping the thigh still, then lower under control. Working one leg at a time makes any imbalance obvious.",
  x_hip_adduction: "Sit in the machine with the pads on the inside of the knees. Squeeze the legs together against the resistance, hold for a beat, then let them open under control to a comfortable stretch. Don't force the stretch at the open position.",
  x_hip_abduction: "Sit with the pads on the outside of the knees. Push the legs apart against the resistance, hold, then return under control. Leaning the torso forward or back changes which part of the glute you feel — experiment.",
  x_machine_calf_raise: "Shoulders or feet in position with the balls of the feet on the platform and heels free. Rise as high as you can onto the toes, pause, then lower until you feel a full stretch in the calf. Bouncing through a tiny range is what makes calf work useless — pause at both ends.",
  x_seated_calf_raise: "Sit with the pad across the thighs just above the knees, balls of the feet on the platform. Rise onto the toes, squeeze, then lower to a deep stretch. The bent knee shifts the work to the soleus, which is why it's a genuinely different exercise from a standing raise.",
  x_barbell_calf_raise: "Bar on the upper back, balls of the feet on a block or plate. Rise as high as possible, pause, then lower slowly to a full stretch. Balance is the limiting factor — use a rack or hold a support.",
  x_dumbbell_calf_raise: "A dumbbell in one hand, other hand on a support, balls of the feet on a block. Rise up, pause at the top, and lower to a deep stretch. Easier to load one leg at a time than with a bar.",
  x_donkey_calf_raise: "Hinge forward at the hips supporting your upper body, balls of the feet on a block, with weight over your hips. Rise onto the toes, pause, lower to a stretch. The hinged position lengthens the calf and is why the old-school lifters swore by it.",

  // --- Biceps ----------------------------------------------------------------
  x_barbell_curl: "Stand tall, bar at arm's length with an underhand shoulder-width grip. Curl by bending the elbows only, keeping them pinned at your sides, then lower all the way to straight. Swinging the hips to launch the bar is the universal fault — if you need it, the weight is too heavy.",
  x_dumbbell_curl: "Dumbbells at your sides, palms forward. Curl one or both, elbows staying beside the ribs, and squeeze at the top before lowering to a full stretch. Let the arms straighten completely at the bottom; stopping short keeps constant tension but costs you the range that builds the muscle.",
  x_ez_bar_curl: "Take the angled grips of an EZ bar, which turns the wrists slightly in and takes the strain off them. Curl with the elbows fixed at your sides, squeeze, and lower under control to straight. The go-to if straight-bar curls make your wrists ache.",
  x_hammer_curl: "Hold the dumbbells with a neutral grip, palms facing each other, and keep them that way throughout. Curl to the shoulder, squeeze, and lower fully. The neutral grip hits the brachialis and the forearm, which is what builds arm thickness rather than just the biceps peak.",
  x_incline_hammer_curl: "Sit back on an incline bench with arms hanging and a neutral grip. Curl without letting the elbows drift forward, then lower to a full stretch. The incline puts the biceps on stretch at the bottom, which is the hardest and most productive part.",
  x_preacher_curl: "Set the pad under your armpits so the upper arms lie flat on it. Curl from a full stretch to the top, then lower slowly all the way down. The pad makes cheating impossible. Never let the weight drop fast at the bottom — that's how biceps tendons get strained.",
  x_spider_curl: "Lie chest-down on an incline bench with the arms hanging straight down. Curl up, squeeze hard, and lower fully. Because the arms hang vertically, the tension peaks at the top of the curl rather than the middle — pause there.",
  x_incline_dumbbell_curl: "Sit back on a 45–60° incline, arms hanging straight down behind the torso. Curl without the elbows travelling forward, then lower to a complete stretch. This is the biggest stretch you can put a biceps under, so keep the weight modest.",
  x_seated_dumbbell_curl: "Sit upright on a bench, dumbbells at your sides. Curl with the elbows fixed, squeeze at the top, lower fully. Sitting removes the ability to swing the hips, which makes it stricter than standing.",
  x_dumbbell_concentration_curl: "Sit, brace the working elbow against the inside of the same-side thigh, and let the dumbbell hang. Curl to the shoulder, squeeze hard, and lower to full extension. The braced elbow makes any cheating impossible, so it's a genuine isolation exercise.",
  x_cable_bicep_curl: "Stand facing a low pulley with a bar or handle. Curl with elbows pinned at the sides, squeeze, and lower under control. Cables keep tension on the biceps at both ends of the movement, which free weights lose at the bottom.",
  x_cable_hammer_curl: "Use a rope on a low pulley, palms facing each other. Curl up keeping the neutral grip, squeeze, and lower fully. Constant cable tension plus the neutral grip makes this excellent for the brachialis.",
  x_incline_cable_curl: "Set an incline bench in front of a low pulley so your arms hang back behind the torso. Curl up, squeeze, lower to the stretch. Combines the incline's stretch with the cable's constant tension.",
  x_lying_cable_curl: "Lie on the floor facing a low pulley, arms extended overhead toward it. Curl the handle toward your forehead, keeping the upper arms still. An unusual angle that keeps the biceps loaded at full stretch.",
  x_overhead_cable_curl: "Stand between two high pulleys, arms out at shoulder height. Curl the handles toward your ears, squeezing hard at the top. The overhead position peaks the biceps under tension in the shortened range — light weight, hard squeeze.",
  x_machine_bicep_curl: "Set the seat so the upper arms lie flat on the pad and the elbows line up with the machine's pivot. Curl up, squeeze, and lower under control to full extension. The fixed path means you can push to failure safely.",
  x_reverse_barbell_curl: "Take an overhand grip and curl, keeping the elbows at your sides and the wrists straight. Expect to use far less weight than a normal curl. It builds the forearm and brachialis, and it's the version that fixes weak wrists.",
  x_dumbbell_reverse_curl: "Dumbbells with an overhand grip, elbows fixed at the sides. Curl up keeping the wrists neutral, then lower fully. Much harder than a standard curl — go light and don't let the wrists collapse.",
  x_zottman_curl: "Curl up with palms facing up, then rotate the wrists at the top so the palms face down, and lower in that reversed position. Rotate back at the bottom. You get a strong curl on the way up and a hard forearm-biased eccentric on the way down.",
  x_strict_curl: "Stand with your back and glutes against a wall and curl a barbell without letting your back, elbows or hips move at all. The wall makes cheating physically impossible — expect to drop your usual curl weight by a third.",
  x_cheat_curl: "Use a controlled hip drive to help the bar past the sticking point, then lower it as slowly as you can. Deliberate cheating to overload the eccentric — it only works if the lowering is genuinely slow, otherwise it's just bad form with heavy weight.",

  // --- Triceps ---------------------------------------------------------------
  x_tricep_pushdown: "Stand at a high pulley with a bar, elbows pinned to your sides. Push down until the arms are straight, squeeze, then let the bar rise under control to about 90° at the elbow. The elbows must not travel forward or drift out — the moment they move, the chest and shoulders take over.",
  x_tricep_rope_pushdown: "Same as a pushdown but with a rope, and at the bottom pull the two ends apart and slightly back. That separation gets a much stronger contraction than a straight bar allows. Keep the elbows locked to your sides throughout.",
  x_reverse_grip_tricep_pushdown: "Take an underhand grip on a straight bar and push down, elbows tight to the sides. The reversed grip biases the medial head of the triceps. Use a lighter weight than a normal pushdown — the grip is the limiting factor.",
  x_lying_tricep_extension: "Lie on a bench with the bar held over the chest. Bend at the elbows only, lowering the bar toward the forehead or just behind it, then extend back up. Keep the upper arms still and angled slightly back — letting them drift makes it a press.",
  x_lying_dumbbell_tricep_extension: "Lie flat with dumbbells above the chest, palms facing each other. Bend the elbows to lower the weights beside the head, then extend. Dumbbells let each arm work independently and are easier on the elbows than a straight bar.",
  x_dumbbell_tricep_extension: "Hold one dumbbell overhead with both hands, or one in each hand. Lower behind the head by bending the elbows only, keeping them pointing forward and close together, then extend. The overhead position stretches the long head, which is the part most arm training misses.",
  x_seated_dumbbell_tricep_extension: "Sit upright with back support, dumbbell held overhead. Lower behind the head under control, then extend to lockout. Back support stops you leaning back to cheat, which is what makes the seated version stricter.",
  x_cable_overhead_tricep_extension: "Face away from a high or low pulley with a rope held behind your head, elbows pointing forward. Extend the arms overhead, then return under control to a full stretch. Constant cable tension on the long head — the best overhead triceps option there is.",
  x_machine_tricep_extension: "Set the seat so the elbows sit at the machine's pivot. Extend the arms against the resistance, squeeze at lockout, and return under control. Safe to take to failure since the path is fixed.",
  x_tricep_extension: "With the elbows fixed and pointing forward, extend the arms against the resistance until straight, squeeze, then return under control to a full bend. Wherever the load comes from, the rule is the same: only the forearm moves.",
  x_dumbbell_tricep_kickback: "Hinge forward with the upper arm parallel to the torso and locked there. Extend the forearm back until the arm is straight, squeeze hard, then return under control. The upper arm never moves. Light weight — this is a peak-contraction exercise, not a strength one.",
  x_cable_kickback: "Same as a dumbbell kickback but on a low pulley, which keeps tension right through to lockout. Hinge forward, upper arm fixed, extend the forearm back and squeeze.",
  x_jm_press: "A hybrid of a close-grip bench and a skull crusher: lower the bar toward the upper chest and throat with the elbows travelling forward, then press back. Keep it light while you learn the groove — it's a favourite of powerlifters for building lockout strength.",
  x_tate_press: "Lie on a bench with dumbbells above the chest, palms facing forward. Lower them by flaring the elbows out and bringing the weights toward the chest in an arcing path, then press back. Odd-feeling but excellent for the triceps at lockout — start very light.",
  x_close_grip_incline_bench_press: "Incline bench, grip about shoulder width, elbows tucked. Lower to the upper chest and press. Combines upper-chest work with a strong triceps demand.",
  x_dips: "Support yourself on parallel bars with arms locked. Lower until the upper arms are about parallel to the floor, then press back up. Stay upright for triceps, lean forward for chest. Going deeper than shoulder comfort allows is the most common way people hurt themselves on these.",
  x_ring_dips: "Dips on gymnastic rings, which move — so you must actively stabilise. Lower under control to about parallel, then press up and turn the rings out at the top. Far harder than bar dips; build on bars first.",
  x_bench_dips: "Hands on a bench behind you, legs extended in front. Lower by bending the elbows until the upper arms are about parallel, then press back up. Keep the back close to the bench. This position is hard on the front of the shoulder, so don't go excessively deep.",
  x_seated_dip_machine: "Sit with the back supported and the handles beside you. Press down until the arms are almost straight, squeeze, then return under control. All the triceps work of a dip with none of the shoulder risk, which makes it the sensible substitute.",

  // --- Delts, shrugs, forearms ----------------------------------------------
  x_dumbbell_lateral_raise: "Stand with dumbbells at your sides, a soft fixed bend in the elbows. Raise out to the sides to about shoulder height, leading with the elbows rather than the hands, then lower slowly. Swinging heavy dumbbells up with the traps is the classic error — this needs light weight and strict control.",
  x_cable_lateral_raise: "Stand side-on to a low pulley, cable across the body, handle in the outside hand. Raise out to shoulder height with a fixed soft elbow, then lower under control. The cable loads the bottom of the movement, which dumbbells barely do.",
  x_machine_lateral_raise: "Sit with the pads against the outside of the upper arms. Raise the arms out to shoulder height, squeeze, and lower under control. The fixed path removes the swinging that ruins most lateral raises.",
  x_dumbbell_front_raise: "Dumbbells in front of the thighs, palms down. Raise one or both straight out in front to shoulder height with a soft elbow, then lower under control. Stop at shoulder height — going higher shifts the work to the traps.",
  x_barbell_front_raise: "Hold a barbell at your thighs with an overhand grip. Raise it straight out to shoulder height with soft elbows, then lower slowly. Leaning back to swing it up is the fault; keep the ribs down and the core braced.",
  x_upright_row: "Hold a bar at your thighs and pull it straight up the front of your body, leading with the elbows, to about chest height. Use a grip at least shoulder width and don't pull above the chest — a narrow grip pulled to the chin is a well-known way to pinch the shoulder.",
  x_dumbbell_upright_row: "Dumbbells in front of the thighs. Pull them up the body leading with the elbows, stopping at chest height. Dumbbells let the wrists and shoulders find a more natural path than a fixed bar, which makes this the safer version.",
  x_cable_upright_row: "Stand at a low pulley with a straight bar or rope. Pull up the body leading with the elbows to chest height, then lower under control. The cable keeps the tension consistent through the pull.",
  x_barbell_shrug: "Hold the bar at arm's length in front of or behind you. Shrug the shoulders straight up toward the ears, hold for a beat, then lower fully. Rolling the shoulders is pointless and hard on the joint — the movement is straight up and straight down.",
  x_dumbbell_shrug: "Dumbbells at your sides at arm's length. Shrug straight up, pause at the top, then lower to a full stretch. Dumbbells sit beside the body rather than in front, which is a more natural line for the traps.",
  x_hex_bar_shrug: "Stand inside a trap bar and shrug straight up. Because the handles are beside you rather than in front, this is the most comfortable heavy shrug available and lets you use the most weight.",
  x_machine_shrug: "Handles at your sides, shrug up, pause, lower under control. The fixed path means you can load it heavily without balance being the limit.",
  x_smith_machine_shrug: "Bar unlocked at thigh height, shrug straight up, pause, lower. The machine takes the balance out entirely so all you do is shrug — a good way to overload the traps safely.",
  x_cable_shrug: "Stand at a low pulley holding a bar or handles. Shrug up, hold, and lower under control. Constant tension through the full range.",
  x_wrist_curl: "Sit with your forearms on your thighs or a bench, palms up, wrists hanging past the edge. Let the bar roll to the fingertips, then curl it back up and squeeze. Only the wrists move. High reps and light weight — the forearms respond to volume.",
  x_dumbbell_wrist_curl: "Forearm braced on the thigh, palm up, dumbbell in hand. Let the wrist extend fully, then curl it up and squeeze. One side at a time allows a fuller range than a bar.",
  x_reverse_wrist_curl: "Forearms braced, palms facing down. Raise the backs of the hands toward you against the resistance, then lower under control. Much weaker than the palm-up version — use a fraction of the weight and expect it to feel awkward.",

  // --- Pullovers -------------------------------------------------------------
  x_dumbbell_pullover: "Lie on a bench holding one dumbbell above the chest with both hands. Keeping a soft fixed elbow bend, lower it back over and behind your head until you feel a stretch across the lats and ribcage, then pull it back over the chest. Control the stretch — this puts the shoulder in a vulnerable position at the bottom.",
  x_barbell_pullover: "Lie on a bench with a barbell above the chest, soft elbows. Lower it back over the head to a strong stretch, then pull it back. The bar is harder to control at the stretch than a dumbbell, so start light and never let it drop.",

  // --- Olympic and explosive -------------------------------------------------
  x_clean: "From the floor, pull the bar past the knees keeping it close to the body, then extend hips, knees and ankles explosively and pull yourself under it, catching it on the front of the shoulders in a squat. Stand up. Learn the catch and the front-rack position with an empty bar before loading it — this is a skill, not a strength lift.",
  x_hang_clean: "Start standing with the bar at mid-thigh. Hinge slightly to lower it to just above the knee, then explosively extend the hips and pull under to catch it on the shoulders. Removing the floor makes it easier to learn the second pull, which is where the power actually comes from.",
  x_hang_power_clean: "As a hang clean, but catch the bar with the hips above parallel rather than dropping into a full squat. Less mobility required and easier to learn, which is why it's the usual entry point for team-sport athletes.",
  x_clean_and_press: "Clean the bar to the shoulders, stand fully, then strictly press it overhead. Two distinct movements — finish the clean and stabilise before starting the press rather than blending them into one heave.",
  x_clean_and_jerk: "Clean the bar to the shoulders and stand. Then dip, drive, and split or push under the bar to receive it locked out overhead, before recovering to standing. Two lifts in one, and both are technical — get coaching rather than learning it from a video.",
  x_clean_pull: "Set up as for a clean and pull the bar as high as possible with the arms straight, finishing on the toes with the hips fully extended. No catch. It builds the pulling strength for the clean without the technical demand of receiving it.",
  x_clean_high_pull: "As a clean pull, but continue pulling with the arms so the bar reaches upper-chest height, elbows high and outside. It bridges the gap between a pull and a full clean.",
  x_snatch: "From the floor with a wide grip, pull the bar close to the body, extend explosively, and pull yourself under to catch it locked out overhead in a squat. Stand up. The most technical lift in the gym — it needs coaching and months of practice with a light bar.",
  x_power_snatch: "As a snatch, but receive the bar overhead with the hips above parallel rather than in a full squat. Less mobility needed than a full snatch while still training the same explosive pull.",
  x_snatch_pull: "Snatch-width grip, pull the bar as high as you can with straight arms, finishing tall on the toes with the hips fully extended. All the pulling power of the snatch with none of the catch.",
  x_dumbbell_snatch: "Start with a dumbbell between your feet. Explosively extend hips and knees, pulling it close to the body, and punch your hand through to catch it locked out overhead in one movement. Far easier to learn than a barbell snatch and a genuinely useful power exercise.",
  x_dumbbell_high_pull: "From a hang at the thighs, explosively extend the hips and pull the dumbbells up to chest height with the elbows high. The hips do the work; the arms just finish it.",
  x_dumbbell_push_press: "Dumbbells at the shoulders. Dip a few inches with the torso vertical, drive hard through the legs, and use that momentum to punch the weights overhead. Reset at the shoulders between reps.",
  x_dumbbell_clean_and_press: "Clean the dumbbells from a hang or the floor to the shoulders, stand tall, then press overhead. Lower to the shoulders and then to the start under control rather than dropping them.",
  x_thruster: "Hold the bar in a front rack, squat to full depth, then use the drive out of the squat to press it straight overhead in one continuous movement. The squat and press must flow together — pausing at the top of the squat wastes all the leg drive and makes it twice as hard.",
  x_dumbbell_thruster: "Dumbbells at the shoulders, squat to depth, then drive up and press them overhead in one motion. Return them to the shoulders and go straight into the next squat.",
  x_wall_ball: "Hold a medicine ball at the chest, squat to full depth, then drive up and throw it to a target on the wall. Catch it as it drops and go straight into the next squat. Absorb the catch by letting it travel down into the next rep rather than fighting it with the arms.",
  x_squat_jump: "From an athletic stance, dip into a quarter or half squat and jump as high as you can. Land soft with bent knees and reset. Quality over quantity — once the jumps get noticeably lower, the set is finished.",
  x_squat_thrust: "From standing, drop into a squat, place the hands on the floor, and kick the feet back into a plank. Jump the feet back in and stand. Keep the hips level as the legs go back — letting them sag loads the lower back.",
  x_burpees: "From standing, drop to the floor, chest down, kick the legs back, then push up, jump the feet in and jump up with the hands overhead. Keep the trunk braced so the hips don't sag when the legs go back — that's where the lower back complains.",

  // --- Push-up variations ----------------------------------------------------
  x_push_ups: "Hands slightly wider than shoulders, body in a straight line from head to heels. Lower until the chest is just off the floor with the elbows at about 45° to the body, then press up. The body stays rigid throughout — sagging hips or a lifted bum means the core has given up before the chest has.",
  x_incline_push_up: "Hands on a bench or bar with the body straight. Lower the chest to the surface and press up. The higher the hands, the easier it is — the best way to build to a full push-up rather than doing bad ones on the floor.",
  x_decline_push_up: "Feet elevated on a bench, hands on the floor. Lower the chest under control and press up, keeping the body rigid. The decline shifts more bodyweight onto the arms and biases the upper chest.",
  x_close_grip_push_up: "Hands about shoulder width or narrower, elbows tucked tight to the ribs as you lower. Press back up. The tucked elbow is what makes it a triceps exercise rather than a chest one.",
  x_diamond_push_ups: "Hands together under the chest with thumbs and index fingers forming a diamond. Lower with the elbows tucked and press up. Hard on the wrists for some — if it pinches, use a close-grip push-up instead.",
  x_one_arm_push_ups: "Feet wide for stability, one hand behind your back. Lower under control with the working elbow tucked, keeping the hips as square as you can, then press up. The anti-rotation demand is enormous — build up with elevated one-arm push-ups first.",
  x_archer_push_ups: "Hands very wide. Lower toward one hand, keeping the other arm straight out to the side, then press back up and alternate. A genuine stepping stone toward a one-arm push-up.",

  // --- Core ------------------------------------------------------------------
  x_crunches: "Lie on your back, knees bent, hands by the ears without pulling on the neck. Curl the shoulders off the floor by shortening the abs, hold for a beat, then lower under control. It's a short movement — dragging your head up with your hands is the fault.",
  x_sit_ups: "Lie with knees bent and feet anchored or flat. Curl the whole torso up to sitting, then lower under control. Roll up through the spine rather than jerking off the floor with a straight back, which throws the load onto the hip flexors and lower back.",
  x_decline_sit_up: "Secure your feet on a decline bench and lie back. Curl up to sitting, then lower under control. The decline adds range and resistance — control the lowering rather than dropping back.",
  x_russian_twist: "Sit with knees bent and lean back to about 45°, feet on or just off the floor. Rotate the torso side to side, touching the floor or a weight beside each hip. Rotate through the trunk rather than just swinging the arms across.",
  x_bicycle_crunch: "Lie on your back, hands by the ears. Bring one knee in and rotate the opposite shoulder toward it, then switch, as if pedalling. Rotate with the ribs rather than yanking the elbow — the elbow touching the knee is not the point.",
  x_side_crunch: "Lie on your side, knees bent, and crunch the ribs toward the hip on the same side, then lower under control. A small movement targeting the obliques — range is naturally short.",
  x_decline_crunch: "On a decline bench with feet secured, curl the shoulders up toward the hips, hold, and lower. Keep it a crunch rather than a full sit-up to keep the tension on the abs.",
  x_reverse_crunches: "Lie on your back with the knees bent at 90°. Curl the hips off the floor, bringing the knees toward the chest, then lower under control. Use the abs to lift the hips rather than swinging the legs for momentum.",
  x_lying_leg_raise: "Lie flat with the legs straight and hands under the hips for support. Raise the legs to vertical, then lower slowly without letting the lower back arch off the floor. The moment the back arches, stop the set — that's the abs giving out.",
  x_flutter_kicks: "Lie on your back, legs straight and just off the floor. Alternate small, quick up-and-down kicks. Press the lower back into the floor throughout; if it arches, raise the legs higher until it doesn't.",
  x_scissor_kicks: "Lie on your back, legs straight and off the floor. Cross one leg over the other and alternate in a scissoring motion. Keep the lower back pressed down and the movement controlled.",
  x_side_leg_raise: "Lie on your side, body in a straight line. Raise the top leg as high as it will go without the hips rolling back, then lower under control. Rolling backward to get more height is the standard fault — keep the hips stacked.",
  x_hanging_leg_raise: "Hang from a bar. With the legs straight, raise them to horizontal or higher by curling the pelvis up, then lower under control without swinging. If you're swinging, do the knee-raise version until you can control it.",
  x_hanging_knee_raise: "Hang from a bar and raise the knees toward the chest, curling the pelvis at the top, then lower under control. The easier progression toward a full leg raise — get the swing out before adding range.",
  x_toes_to_bar: "Hang from a bar and bring the toes up to touch it, then lower under control. It needs hamstring flexibility and strong abs together. Use a controlled kip if you're doing them for reps, but learn the strict version first.",
  x_ab_wheel_rollout: "Kneel holding the wheel under the shoulders. Roll forward as far as you can while keeping the hips and ribs locked in a straight line, then pull back. The instant your lower back arches, you've gone too far — shorten the range rather than pushing through.",
  x_cable_crunch: "Kneel facing a high pulley with a rope beside your head. Crunch down by curling the spine and bringing the elbows toward the thighs, then return under control. The hips stay still — if you're hinging at the hip it's become a pulldown.",
  x_standing_cable_crunch: "Stand facing away from a high pulley with the rope behind your head. Crunch forward by curling the ribs toward the hips, then return. Keep the hips fixed and bend the spine, not the waist.",
  x_machine_seated_crunch: "Sit with the pad against the chest and grip the handles. Crunch forward by shortening the abs, hold, then return under control. The fixed path makes it easy to load the abs progressively, which most core work never does.",
  x_cable_woodchopper: "Set a pulley high, take the handle in both hands, and pull it down and across the body to the opposite hip while rotating the torso. Return under control. Rotate through the trunk with the hips relatively stable — that resistance to rotation is the exercise.",
  x_dumbbell_side_bend: "Hold one dumbbell at your side and bend directly sideways at the waist, lowering it down your leg, then come back to upright. Only one weight — holding one in each hand cancels the load out entirely, which is the most common mistake here.",
  x_superman: "Lie face-down with arms extended overhead. Raise the arms, chest and legs off the floor together, hold for a beat, then lower. A gentle lower-back and glute exercise — lift to a comfortable height rather than cranking into a big arch.",

  // --- Conditioning ----------------------------------------------------------
  x_jumping_jack: "From standing, jump the feet out wide while raising the arms overhead, then jump back in and lower the arms. Land softly on the balls of the feet with the knees slightly bent. A warm-up staple that raises the heart rate without needing any space or kit.",
  x_mountain_climbers: "From a push-up position with the body straight, drive one knee toward the chest and switch feet rapidly. Keep the hips low and level — letting them pop up turns it into a stretch instead of core and conditioning work.",
};
